<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Yarlung Valley</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Yarlung Valley is a valley south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yarlung-tsangpo">Yarlung Tsangpo</a> river in Lhoka, south-central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tibet">Tibet</a>, around the town of Tsetang. It was the home of the line of chiefs who united Tibet in the seventh century and founded the Tibetan Empire, and the neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chongye">Chongye</a> valley holds their burial mounds. It later became the seat of the Phagmodrupa rulers at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nedong">Nedong</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yarlung-valley.jpg" alt="Yarlung Valley" width="320" height="240" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: MyName (Fanghong) · CC BY 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:YumbuLhakhang.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Tibetan Empire grew out of the <b>Yarlung</b> valley in south-central Tibet, where a line of chiefs later remembered as the first kings of Tibet held lands south of the Yarlung Tsangpo river.</blockquote><a href="/empire/tibetan-empire">Tibetan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>By 1354 he controlled most of central Tibet and ruled from Nedong in the <b>Yarlung</b> valley.</blockquote><a href="/state/phagmodrupa-dynasty">Phagmodrupa dynasty</a></li><li><blockquote>Old Tibetan annals treat it as a major rival of the <b>Yarlung</b> kings.</blockquote><a href="/state/zhangzhung">Zhangzhung</a></li></ul></section>
